steps.text:
**Castling** is the only move with two pieces. The king moves **two squares** toward a rook, and that rook jumps to the square the king passed over. Kingside: **O-O** (king to g1, rook to f1). Queenside: **O-O-O** (king to c1, rook to d1).

You may castle only if:
- neither the king nor that rook has moved before;
- all squares between them are empty;
- your king is **not in check**, does not **pass through** an attacked square, and does not **land on** an attacked square.

Why castle? It tucks the king behind pawns and brings the rook toward the center. In almost every game you should castle within the first ten moves.

**Your own king.** After castling, keep the pawn shield (f2, g2, h2) mostly unmoved — every pawn move in front of the king creates weak squares. Keep at least one minor piece near the king (a knight on f3 is the best defender of h2 and h7). Do not let the enemy open files toward your king.

**Attacking the enemy king.** Signs that an attack might work:
- more of your pieces near the enemy king than defenders;
- weaknesses in the pawn shield (a pawn on g6 or h6, a missing f-pawn);
- the enemy king still in the center;
- your pawn on e5 kicking away the knight from f6 (the best defender).

**Same-side castling**: attack with pieces; pushing your own pawns weakens your king.
**Opposite-side castling**: it is a race. Throw your pawns at the enemy king (a **pawn storm**) to open files — whoever opens lines first usually wins.
